1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,247
Six kilometres under the sea, the water will squeeze this titanium ball — with almost one lakh tonnes.

2
00:00:07,247 --> 00:00:10,496
Imagine you're inside it, with two other Indians.

3
00:00:10,496 --> 00:00:14,902
This ball is the heart of India's first deep-sea sub for people.

4
00:00:14,902 --> 00:00:16,960
Its name is Matsya.

5
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:22,108
Your whole world is five pieces of titanium, joined by seven welds.

6
00:00:22,108 --> 00:00:23,801
Now here's the strange part.

7
00:00:23,801 --> 00:00:25,781
Isro is known for rockets.

8
00:00:25,781 --> 00:00:28,774
But Isro's hardest weld wasn't for a rocket.

9
00:00:28,774 --> 00:00:30,416
It was for this ball.

10
00:00:30,416 --> 00:00:35,015
That weld was four times thicker than their welding machine could handle before.

11
00:00:35,015 --> 00:00:37,502
It took about seven hundred tries.

12
00:00:37,502 --> 00:00:39,172
So why was it so hard?

13
00:00:39,172 --> 00:00:42,231
And after Titan, how will they know it holds?

14
00:00:42,231 --> 00:00:44,300
By the end, you'll know both.

15
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,028
First, I want you to feel what six kilometres of sea actually does.

16
00:00:49,028 --> 00:00:55,077
Every ten metres you go down, the sea presses on you as hard as all the air above you.

17
00:00:55,077 --> 00:00:56,096
Once more.

18
00:00:56,096 --> 00:00:57,184
And again.

19
00:00:57,184 --> 00:00:58,643
And again.

20
00:00:58,643 --> 00:01:01,859
At six thousand metres, hold out your finger.

21
00:01:01,859 --> 00:01:05,915
The water would press on your fingernail with about six hundred kilos.

22
00:01:05,915 --> 00:01:09,680
That's a full-grown buffalo, standing on one fingernail.

23
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:12,204
Now cover the whole ball with fingernails.

24
00:01:12,204 --> 00:01:15,021
Every one of them carries a buffalo.

25
00:01:15,021 --> 00:01:22,278
Add it all up, and the sea squeezes this ball with almost one lakh tonnes, from every side at once.

26
00:01:22,278 --> 00:01:25,329
So you're probably asking the same question I asked.

27
00:01:25,329 --> 00:01:27,267
Could this end like Titan?

28
00:01:27,267 --> 00:01:28,516
You may remember it.

29
00:01:28,516 --> 00:01:33,453
In June twenty twenty-three, Titan broke apart on its way down to the Titanic.

30
00:01:33,453 --> 00:01:35,210
Five people died.

31
00:01:35,210 --> 00:01:38,410
Its hull, as you may know, was carbon fibre.

32
00:01:38,410 --> 00:01:44,006
After earlier dives, its layers had started to come apart, and it kept diving anyway.

33
00:01:44,006 --> 00:01:47,925
The U-S Coast Guard said the company's own failures caused it.

34
00:01:47,925 --> 00:01:49,803
So what's different here?

35
00:01:49,803 --> 00:01:50,797
Three things.

36
00:01:50,797 --> 00:01:56,230
The material is titanium, the metal deep-sea subs have trusted for more than fifty years.

37
00:01:56,230 --> 00:01:58,001
The shape is a ball.

38
00:01:58,001 --> 00:02:01,975
And an outside inspection company checks and approves the work.

39
00:02:01,975 --> 00:02:04,666
For Matsya, that company is from Norway.

40
00:02:04,666 --> 00:02:06,937
And if something goes wrong down there?

41
00:02:06,937 --> 00:02:10,571
The crew can drop heavy weights, and the sub rises back up.

42
00:02:10,571 --> 00:02:13,767
And they carry enough air for ninety-six hours.

43
00:02:13,767 --> 00:02:15,255
But why a ball?

44
00:02:15,255 --> 00:02:18,036
Why not a long tube, like a normal submarine?

45
00:02:18,036 --> 00:02:19,701
Squeeze a ball in your hand.

46
00:02:19,701 --> 00:02:22,566
You can feel the push spread all the way round.

47
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:26,093
There's no corner and no flat side where the pressure can win.

48
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:29,843
Even America's famous deep-sea sub, Alvin, uses a ball.

49
00:02:29,843 --> 00:02:32,987
Its first one, in nineteen sixty-four, was steel.

50
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:38,893
In nineteen seventy-three, they changed it to titanium, and Alvin could go almost twice as deep.

51
00:02:38,893 --> 00:02:40,650
India walked the same road.

52
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:46,701
In twenty twenty-one, it lowered a test ball of plain steel six hundred metres into the Bay of Bengal.

53
00:02:46,701 --> 00:02:48,361
Then came titanium.

54
00:02:48,361 --> 00:02:51,047
And this, you see, is where Isro comes in.

55
00:02:51,047 --> 00:02:53,034
Remember that weld I told you about?

56
00:02:53,034 --> 00:02:56,022
You can't make a ball this big in one piece.

57
00:02:56,022 --> 00:02:57,553
So how do you make it?

58
00:02:57,553 --> 00:02:59,928
Isro's drawing shows five pieces.

59
00:02:59,928 --> 00:03:00,963
A top hatch.

60
00:03:00,963 --> 00:03:01,926
Three rings.

61
00:03:01,926 --> 00:03:03,033
A bottom dome.

62
00:03:03,033 --> 00:03:06,548
Together, they weigh about five and a half tonnes.

63
00:03:06,548 --> 00:03:08,428
Seven welds join them.

64
00:03:08,428 --> 00:03:11,329
Four go right round the ball, like belts.

65
00:03:11,329 --> 00:03:13,391
Three go round its windows.

66
00:03:13,391 --> 00:03:15,166
Here's the problem you'd face.

67
00:03:15,166 --> 00:03:17,276
Titanium is fussy.

68
00:03:17,276 --> 00:03:21,170
Heat it in normal air, and it grabs oxygen and nitrogen.

69
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:25,996
The weld fills with tiny holes, and it becomes weak and easy to crack.

70
00:03:25,996 --> 00:03:28,821
So you can't weld it with a normal flame.

71
00:03:28,821 --> 00:03:29,835
What do you do?

72
00:03:29,835 --> 00:03:34,135
Isro used a beam of electrons, fired inside a vacuum.

73
00:03:34,135 --> 00:03:36,745
No air, so nothing can get in.

74
00:03:36,745 --> 00:03:38,374
But there was a catch.

75
00:03:38,374 --> 00:03:43,008
Isro's welding centre in Bengaluru could weld up to two centimetres thick.

76
00:03:43,008 --> 00:03:44,838
This wall is eight.

77
00:03:44,838 --> 00:03:47,907
So they made their machine much more powerful.

78
00:03:47,907 --> 00:03:49,393
Then they practised.

79
00:03:49,393 --> 00:03:53,946
About seven hundred trial welds, to get the settings exactly right.

80
00:03:53,946 --> 00:03:55,847
Then came the real thing.

81
00:03:55,847 --> 00:03:58,912
One of the big welds that go right round the ball.

82
00:03:58,912 --> 00:04:02,405
Eight centimetres deep, and about seven metres long.

83
00:04:02,405 --> 00:04:05,440
It took thirty-two minutes, in one go.

84
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:10,313
Isro says it was the first time anyone in India had made a weld like this.

85
00:04:10,313 --> 00:04:12,764
But then, how do you know it's good inside?

86
00:04:12,764 --> 00:04:14,678
You can't cut it open, can you?

87
00:04:14,678 --> 00:04:20,348
So they built X-ray machines strong enough to see through eight centimetres of titanium.

88
00:04:20,348 --> 00:04:23,736
They used sound waves too, listening for hidden cracks.

89
00:04:23,736 --> 00:04:26,919
And an outside agency has to certify it.

90
00:04:26,919 --> 00:04:28,803
Now step inside with me.

91
00:04:28,803 --> 00:04:34,018
The wall is eight centimetres thick, so inside, you get just over two metres.

92
00:04:34,018 --> 00:04:39,423
Lie down in there, and your head and your feet would almost touch the walls.

93
00:04:39,423 --> 00:04:43,869
You and two others sit here, for a normal dive of twelve hours.

94
00:04:43,869 --> 00:04:45,208
Oxygen comes in.

95
00:04:45,208 --> 00:04:48,380
The carbon dioxide you breathe out gets cleaned away.

96
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:50,818
And who will sit where you're sitting?

97
00:04:50,818 --> 00:04:57,425
In August twenty twenty-five, India sent its first two trained people down in a French sub called Nautile.

98
00:04:57,425 --> 00:05:01,612
Raju Ramesh, a scientist, reached about four thousand metres.

99
00:05:01,612 --> 00:05:07,540
The next day, Commander Jatinder Pal Singh, a retired Navy officer, reached about five thousand.

100
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:10,192
So is Matsya ready, do you think?

101
00:05:10,192 --> 00:05:11,120
Not quite.

102
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:12,814
There's one more problem.

103
00:05:12,814 --> 00:05:15,250
This ball is only part of the sub.

104
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:19,105
Around it go batteries, lights, motors and arms.

105
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:22,754
Put it all together, and the whole thing sinks like a stone.

106
00:05:22,754 --> 00:05:25,279
So it's wrapped in a special foam.

107
00:05:25,279 --> 00:05:30,015
The foam is full of tiny hollow glass bubbles, set in hard plastic.

108
00:05:30,015 --> 00:05:32,001
The titanium takes the squeeze.

109
00:05:32,001 --> 00:05:34,205
The foam brings everyone home.

110
00:05:34,205 --> 00:05:38,043
That foam had to come from France, and it arrived late.

111
00:05:38,043 --> 00:05:42,764
Then, early this year, officials said a small crack was found in its cover.

112
00:05:42,764 --> 00:05:46,292
That held up the first test dive, to five hundred metres.

113
00:05:46,292 --> 00:05:51,878
On the sixth of November, Matsya finished another round of trials near Chennai, without people inside.

114
00:05:51,878 --> 00:05:56,918
Officials have said the full six kilometre dive could come in twenty twenty-seven.

115
00:05:56,918 --> 00:05:59,138
So why do all this, you ask?

116
00:05:59,138 --> 00:06:01,920
What's down there that's worth it?

117
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:05,861
Six thousand metres down, you'd see no sunlight at all.

118
00:06:05,861 --> 00:06:09,451
Your lights are the only lights for six kilometres.

119
00:06:09,451 --> 00:06:13,158
Outside your window, the water is close to freezing.

120
00:06:13,158 --> 00:06:17,693
And the seafloor is covered with black lumps of metal, like stones.

121
00:06:17,693 --> 00:06:23,296
India has the right to explore a big patch of that seabed, in the middle of the Indian Ocean.

122
00:06:23,296 --> 00:06:26,102
It has held that right since two thousand and two.

123
00:06:26,102 --> 00:06:31,581
In twenty twenty-two, an Indian robot sub surveyed it, more than five kilometres down.

124
00:06:31,581 --> 00:06:34,796
Those lumps hold nickel, copper and cobalt.

125
00:06:34,796 --> 00:06:37,918
Metals that go into batteries, like the one in your phone.

126
00:06:37,918 --> 00:06:39,792
But let me be honest with you.

127
00:06:39,792 --> 00:06:42,248
Nobody is allowed to mine there yet.

128
00:06:42,248 --> 00:06:44,498
The world is still writing the rules.

129
00:06:44,498 --> 00:06:46,760
Matsya is not a mining machine.

130
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:48,168
It's a pair of eyes.

131
00:06:48,168 --> 00:06:50,774
And you might ask, what about China?

132
00:06:50,774 --> 00:06:56,892
Its sub has already taken people to the deepest point on Earth, almost eleven thousand metres.

133
00:06:56,892 --> 00:07:02,087
India's first goal is six thousand, deep enough for the seabed it wants to explore.

134
00:07:02,087 --> 00:07:05,625
Now, one last time, come back inside the ball with me.

135
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:06,865
I'll be straight with you.

136
00:07:06,865 --> 00:07:10,086
Matsya has not been to sea with people in it yet.

137
00:07:10,086 --> 00:07:15,403
And no public report says this titanium ball has been tested at full pressure yet.

138
00:07:15,403 --> 00:07:18,340
So the last test will be the sea itself.

139
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:19,380
One dive.

140
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:20,712
Six kilometres.

141
00:07:20,712 --> 00:07:23,678
Every number in this film, checked at once.

142
00:07:23,678 --> 00:07:25,382
This story isn't over.

143
00:07:25,382 --> 00:07:28,639
When Matsya makes its first deep dive, we'll be there.

144
00:07:28,639 --> 00:07:31,120
Subscribe to Be Practical, so you're there too.

